    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d96">

                <pb facs="d96-01" n="1" xml:id="d96-01"/>

                <head>96. Notes of Telephone Conversation Between the President’s Assistant for
                    National Security Affairs (Kissinger) and the Deputy Secretary of Defense (Packard)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d96-f1"/>
                    <anchor n="2" xml:id="d96-f2"/>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1969-05-06T10:40:00-04:00">May 6, 1969, 10:40 a.m.</date>
                </dateline>
                <p>
                    K said he was calling Mel but could not reach him
                    and asked if P was familiar with Seabed problem.
                        P said just vaguely.K said Mel opposed it at NSC but
                    called K after and said if we had to proceed he
                    would like to make sure it contained some amendments. K said he wondered if he could get these changes. P said the main thing is that there is nothing in the
                    draft that would jeopardize their underwater capability. K said President will probably go ahead with treaty but on the
                    condition that Gerry Smith puts into the draft the changes Defense wants. P asked when K wanted
                    it and K said by tomorrow morning. K said he is seeing Gerry Smith at 6:00p tomorrow
                    night at his office. President wants K to
                    negotiate this. P said K would have them by morning.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d96-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d96-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Library of Congress, Manuscript Division, Kissinger Papers, Box 359, Telephone Conversations,
                    Chronological File, 1–14 May 1969. No classification marking.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d96-f2" n="2" xml:id="d96-f2-content">
                <p>
                    Kissinger told Packard the President
                    would probably go ahead with the treaty if Smith put into the draft the changes
                    that DOD wanted.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
